from ../plugins import Plugins
from ./Init import Init

export anchor Initialization:
    description:
        The init plugin, bundled with the compiler, adds `piton init`, which
        starts a new project from a template. It is a plugin, described in
        @{Plugins}, like any other: the compiler has no init command of its
        own.

        Its source is in the compiler's repository at plugins/init: its
        declaration in plugin.pi, its code in src, and its templates in
        templates. It has no Piton files of its own.

    command: @{Init}

    declaration:
        Its declaration names the init command and its arguments: directory,
        positional; template, short t; adapter, short a, which can be given
        more than once; list, a flag that can't be given with the others; and
        no-tether, a flag. The command is standalone, so it runs outside a
        project, and inside one whose configuration has errors.

    capabilities:
        It may ask the user on the terminal, which is interactive, and start
        new projects, which is scaffold: write the template's files into the
        directory the user names, never over a file, and tether the new
        project's dependencies. It reads no project files and runs no
        programs.

    templates:
        The templates are in the templates directory of the plugin, one
        directory for each group and one in it for each template, as @{Init}
        describes. The plugin lists them with its resources, and reads each
        file as a resource. Authors edit a template by editing its files, and
        add one by adding a directory with a `.template` file beside its
        files; nothing else changes.

    adapters:
        The adapters it offers are those @piton/belay exports, each with its
        target id, the name of the anchor exported for it, and the platform's
        name. The plugin keeps that table in its code.
